================================================================
TOPIC 2 — TYPE HINTS AND RUNTIME VALIDATION
CONCEPT 1 — TypeVar AND Generic
================================================================

CORE PURPOSE
------------
TypeVar and Generic allow classes and functions to work with
multiple types while preserving type information end to end.
Without them, containers and wrappers return Any, losing all
type checker and IDE assistance downstream.

TypeVar — BASIC USAGE
---------------------
from typing import TypeVar

T = TypeVar("T")

def identity(x: T) -> T:
    return x

The type checker reads: whatever type goes in, that same type
comes out. No Any, no loss of type information.

Generic — PARAMETERISING A CLASS
---------------------------------
from typing import TypeVar, Generic

T = TypeVar("T")

class EmbeddingStore(Generic[T]):
    def __init__(self) -> None:
        self.embeddings: dict[str, T] = {}

    def add(self, id: str, embedding: T) -> None:
        self.embeddings[id] = embedding

    def get(self, id: str) -> T | None:
        return self.embeddings.get(id)

Usage:
    store: EmbeddingStore[list[float]] = EmbeddingStore()

The type parameter flows through all method calls. The checker
knows get() returns list[float] | None, not T | None or Any.

ANNOTATIONS ARE STATIC — RUNTIME IS SEPARATE
---------------------------------------------
T is a type checker tool only. At runtime:
    - dict[str, T] = {} is a plain empty dict
    - EmbeddingStore[list[float]]() constructs a plain object
    - T is gone the moment __init__ runs

Python never enforces annotations. This is valid at runtime:
    store: EmbeddingStore[list[float]] = EmbeddingStore()
    store.add("1", "wrong type")  # no runtime error

A type checker (mypy/pyright) would flag this. Python ignores it.

bound= — CONSTRAINING TypeVar
------------------------------
T = TypeVar("T", bound=nn.Module)

Means: T can only be nn.Module or a subclass of nn.Module.
Use when your function needs to call specific methods on T.

bound=object means any type is allowed (effectively no constraint).

TypeVar WITH bound= VS PLAIN BASE CLASS ANNOTATION
---------------------------------------------------
# Loses subclass identity — return type is nn.Module
def freeze(model: nn.Module) -> nn.Module: ...

# Preserves subclass identity — return type is whatever was passed in
T = TypeVar("T", bound=nn.Module)
def freeze(model: T) -> T: ...

Use T when the function returns the same object it received and
the caller needs to keep using it as the specific subclass.

Callable — TYPING FUNCTIONS AS ARGUMENTS
-----------------------------------------
from collections.abc import Callable

Callable[[InputType], ReturnType]

Example:
    def transform(self, fn: Callable[[T], S]) -> "Pipeline[S]":

This bridges T and S — the checker infers S from the function
passed in, making the return type Pipeline[S] fully resolved.

Import from collections.abc (Python 3.9+), not typing.

TypeVar MUST BE ANCHORED
-------------------------
A TypeVar appearing only in a return type with no connection to
any parameter is meaningless — equivalent to Any.

    def transform(self, fn) -> "Pipeline[S]":  # S is unanchored — useless

S must be tied to fn's type to be meaningful:
    def transform(self, fn: Callable[[T], S]) -> "Pipeline[S]":

ANNOTATION STYLE CONVENTION
----------------------------
Left-side annotation, plain constructor on the right:
    store: EmbeddingStore[list[float]] = EmbeddingStore()

Right-side form works but is redundant — the generic alias is
discarded immediately after construction.

WHEN TO USE Generic[T]
-----------------------
- Containers holding items of varying type: caches, stores,
  datasets, result wrappers
- Functions returning the same type they receive: model wrappers,
  freeze functions, decorators
- Functions accepting a callable and producing output typed by
  that callable's return type

TWO SEPARATE LAYERS
-------------------
Annotations + type checker — catch mistakes you write, before execution
Pydantic                   — catches bad data that arrives, at runtime

Neither replaces the other. A production ML codebase uses both.

